# SAME70Q20B USB-CDC boot/connect smoke test. Exercises the
# CONFIG_ATSAM_USB firmware variant (duet3-6hc / duet3-6xd printer
# configs are USB+CAN-only on real hardware, no serial-mode firmware
# variant exists) against the local USBHS Renode peripheral
# (test/emulator/repl/same70_usbhs.cs). The peripheral uses a
# "skip-enum cheat" - inject a SET_CONFIGURATION SETUP packet on EP0
# so klipper's CDC stack reaches bulk-active without a full USB host
# emulation - then exposes BULK_IN/BULK_OUT as a UART pipe to the
# host pty via UARTBase. The companion same70_smoke.test covers the
# CONFIG_ATSAM_SERIAL path; both share the same .repl (USBHS is
# resident unconditionally and stays idle when the firmware never
# touches it).
#
# KNOWN BLOCKED (the one remaining EMULATOR test that does not pass
# under --force-emulator). Diagnosis below is from clean-host runs on a
# native-Linux x86_64 box (deterministic, not the disk-pressured macOS
# Docker that masked these as flaky):
#   * At the upstream SysTick rate the skip-enum cheat works end-to-end:
#     enumeration completes, klippy reaches "Loaded MCU 'mcu'" and ~110
#     BULK_IN transfers flow, then the firmware shuts down with
#     static_string_id 56 "Rescheduled timer in the past" (armcm_timer.c
#     timer_dispatch_many: a scheduled timer is >1ms behind DWT->CYCCNT).
#   * It is NOT a SysTick-frequency mismatch: pinning the NVIC
#     systickFrequency to the 300MHz core clock removes the shutdown but
#     deterministically BREAKS enumeration (the firmware then never
#     enables RXSTPES on EP0, so the SETUP cheat never injects). Tried
#     and reverted.
#   * It is NOT IRQ-servicing granularity: a cpu MaximumBlockSize sweep
#     (1..1024 via RENODE_EXP_BLOCKSIZE) leaves the reason-56 shutdown
#     unchanged.
#   * Root cause is the wall-clock vs virtual-time drift: Renode runs the
#     SAM CPU at a rate that diverges from CONFIG_CLOCK_FREQ, so klippy's
#     clocksync (wall-clock paced) computes garbage and the firmware's
#     own USB-build boot timers overshoot. Raising RENODE_EXP_MIPS hides
#     the overshoot but then runs virtual time faster than the
#     wall-clock-paced klippy/USB pty can follow, so enumeration breaks -
#     a genuine two-sided tension. The same drift is why renode homing
#     ("Communication timeout") and the migrated tick tests fail.
#   * The real fix is deterministic tick-mode on ARM, which today
#     livelocks: the byte shuttle is not synchronized with each RunFor
#     (klippy's serialqueue C bg-thread polls in real time while sim time
#     advances in jumps), so klippy re-requests the same advance target
#     forever and identify never frames. Closing this needs a synchronous
#     RunFor<->UART byte shuttle plus a sim-time-aware serialqueue.
DICTIONARY same70q20b-usb.dict
CONFIG same70_usb_smoke.cfg
EMULATOR same70_usb_smoke.fixture.json

# A few harmless gcode lines so klippy gets past klippy:ready.
M114
M105
M105
M105

# klippy connected and stayed connected long enough to do the
# clocksync handshake against the firmware's generic/armcm_timer view
# transported over the synthetic USBHS bulk endpoints.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Loaded MCU 'mcu'"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Internal error"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Klippy is shutdown"
